This document contains the basics for running and using the
PEBIL binary instrumentation package. Direct any questions or
comments to allysonc@epanalytics.com

1. Installation - Please see /path/to/pebil/INSTALL

2. Source PEBIL - Bring the PEBIL into your environment:

      cd /path/to/pebil
      source bashrc

3. Compile the application to be instrumented. The application 
   must be compiled with -g.

4. Instrument the application:

      pebil --tool <ChosenTool> --app <YourApplication> --saveall

   See below for tool descriptions.

   Other options to consider:

      A. --threaded
         Use this option if your application is compiled with 
         OpenMP

      B. --sanitize
         Use this option if your application is sensitive and 
         you would like to share PEBIL output with others

      C. --fbl <file>
         Use this option if you would like to blacklist or 
         whitelist certain functions

         For a blacklist, create a file with the functions 
         you don't want instrumented, one on each line:

             function1
             function2
             function3
             ...

        For a whitelist, create a file with the functions
        you want instrumented, one on each line, and add
        a * at the top of the file:

             *
             function1
             function2
             function3
             ...

5. Tune the tool using environment variables - Some tools 
   have parameters that can be set through the environment.
   For example, if you want the function timer tool to 
   stop timing very short functions, you would set the 
   following variable:

        export FTIMER_SHUTOFF=1

   More information about the possible environment variables 
   is available below.

6. Run the application. PEBIL will produce an instrumented 
   binary, which usually has the name of the application 
   followed by a special suffix (e.g. 
   <YourApplication>.jbbinst). Replace your binary with 
   this instrumented binary, but otherwise run it as normal!

   For example, if you normally run your application like:

        mpirun -np 32 ./theApp -x 4 -y 4 -z 2 < input.txt

   You would run the instrumented application like: 

        mpirun -np 32 ./theApp.jbbinst -x 4 -y 4 -z 2 < input.txt


*********************************************************************

PEBIL Tools


1. Basic Block Counting
   A low-impact tool that reports how often code was executed. 
   Useful for determining where to start performance analysis.

       pebil --tool BasicBlockCounter --app <APP> ...

   Generates an instrumented binary called <APP>.jbbinst

2. Function Timing
   A tool for timing how long the application spends in each 
   function. Useful for performing a hot-spot analysis

      pebil --tool Function Timer --app <APP> --lnc libtimer.so ...

   Generates an instrumented binary called <APP>.ftiminst

   Consider setting the following environment variables:

      A. FTIMER_CPU_FREQ=<FREQ>
         Set the CPU frequency (in Hz).

      B. FTIMER_SHUTOFF=1
         Turn off small functions which may report skewed times 
         due to the overhead of timing the function
   
